{{> Top frame}}
<header>
  <p class="kicker">Lesson 3</p>
  <h1>Types</h1>
  <p class="lede">A template and the Roc that fills it agree by type. Ask for a field Roc
  does not give, and it is a compile error, never a blank on the page.</p>
</header>

<figure class="preview">
  <figcaption><code>templates/Hello.rocstache</code>, rendered</figcaption>
{{> Hello hello}}</figure>

<section class="turn-box">
  <h2>Your turn</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><p>In <code>templates/Hello.rocstache</code>, after the name, add
    <code>from &#123;&#123; city &#125;&#125;</code>. Save.</p>
    <p>roux adds <code>city : Str</code> to the contract in <code>templates/Hello.roc</code>, and roc finds
    that <code>main.roc</code> builds <code>hello</code> without it. The error appears over this
    page, and the page underneath is still the last good build.</p></li>
    <li><p>In <code>main.roc</code>, give it one:</p>
    <pre><code>hello = { name: you, city: "Reykjavík" }</code></pre>
    <p>Save. The error goes, the page reloads, and the box says where you are from.</p></li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Why it works this way</h2>
  <p>A field the template reads that no one provides, a typo, a list where a string was
  meant: each is caught by roc, at the line in your Roc where the record is made. The
  template's side is checked too: roux refuses a tag it cannot read.</p>
  <p>Fields a template infers are strings. For numbers, a template declares its contract at
  the top, as the next lesson's menu does.</p>
</section>
